5. Best Practices when Connecting Tools to Real APIs
Authentication and Security: Always secure API keys and sensitive information (using environment variables instead of hardcoding).
Error Handling: Implement robust error handling for network errors, invalid responses, and API rate limiting.
Modularity: Keep tool modules independent so that agents can easily switch or update external services.
Caching: Consider caching frequent queries to minimize API calls and improve responsiveness.
Testing: Use automated testing and mocks for API calls during development to simulate responses reliably.
6. Common Misconceptions
Direct API Calls Inside Agent Logic: Developers sometimes embed API calls directly within the agent's core logic. It is better to separate this functionality into dedicated tool modules.
Ignoring Rate Limits: Not accounting for API rate limits can result in blocked API access. Implement proper throttling and backoff mechanisms.
Poor Error Handling: Assuming that the API will always return successful responses can lead to crashes. Always code defensively.
Hardcoding API Keys: Embedding API keys in source code is insecure. Instead, make use of environment variables or secure vaults.
Synchronous API Calls: Blocking calls can freeze the agent’s response. Always use asynchronous approaches to handle network I/O.
